terization study that was  conducted to look at  our commonly used strs to understand how  sequence diversity can be of use in  forensic genetics  excuse me so we looked at sequence  variation  for 59 strs 27 autosomal seven  xstr's and 25 y chromosome strs  and this included all of the coracotis  loci  we performed this characterization study  in 777  individuals across four major population  groups  and we utilized the forensic library  preparation kit  in combination with the my seek fgx  forensic genomic system  and we performed testing or our analyses  using both the manufacturer software as  well as a variety of in-house tools  the main goals of this study were really  to examine  what sequence diversity existed in the  commonly used  strs and how significant the sequence  diversity was  further we wanted to understand the  differences between the repeat region  and the flanking region  sequence diversity and how those  two types of variation might come  together to make a locus  or a location in the dna particularly  heterozygous  or diverse we also wanted to observe if  there were any particular trends  in the data whether certain loci were  more  heterozygous using a sequence based  approach and we performed a variety of  population genetic tests to ensure that  the data  were conforming to our expectations and  could be utilized in a forensic setting  we also wanted to identify any  shortcomings or limitations that could  be problematic for other research groups  but ultimately we wanted to just  generate a compendium of data that could  be used  by the forensic community as we move  forward into this new area  what we found in this really extensive  study was  four main trends first we saw an  increase in alleles for loci  when looking at only the repeat region  second  we saw an increase in alleles for  certain loci looking only at the  flanking regions so that region  outside of the repeat region third we  saw an increase in alleles in certain  loci  wh