les  but you can also see with the purple  arrow that there is variation  in that repeat region changing the  allele 12 from being  one allele to now two alleles because  there are two  possible explanations from a sequence  based approach to account for that  repeat region  and then if you go further out to the  orange arrow you can see that there's  flanking  flanking region information taking that  original  length-based allele 12 from one to now  three possibilities  so mps really allows you to enhance  your knowledge about the allele  at every location that you're looking at  so what exactly is sequence variation so  i've shown you a picture but that  doesn't really tell you what it is  we're really talking about any  difference in nucleotide sequence  in the amplicon or dna sequence of  interest  so it can be a single snip or single  nucleotide polymorphism difference  here the example goes from an a  to t or it can be a repeat difference  where the snips have manifested in a  different combination  from one original  motif structure to a new structure so  here the repeat difference  is for an allele length base 7 where it  goes tcta2  tcta tctg3 tcta2  to now tcta 1 tctg4  tcta2 and so you can see that  the combination of that motif has  changed even though the pattern is still  the same  taking that a step further and why this  is really valuable for our  interpretation  is that if you have an individual that  has a 1415 allele  using capillary electrophoresis you're  going to see the 1415  peak demonstrated on  the right side of the screen and so the  14 allele is simply a 14 the 15 allele  is simply a 15.  but if you introduce any type of  sequence variation for either the 14 or  the 15  that now increases the diversity  of those alleles so for example here we  have three potential motifs for the 14  and five potential motifs for the 15  allele  and to take that a step further if you  had two individuals who were both 14  15 by length but one of them  had at least one difference in their  nucleotide sequence at that location  for those alleles you would now go from  those  two individuals having the same genotype  by length to now having unique genotypes  by sequence  and that's extremely useful from a power  of discrimination standpoint and from an  interpretation standpoint for  identifying who contributed those  alleles  to the dna evidence in question  again taking that one step further  and looking at a dna mixture not knowing  that the two individuals are  heterozygous 1415  you're presented with their mixed dna  profile  so on the left using pcrce you would get  the 1415  peaks and you wouldn't necessarily know  how the two component contributors  gave their alleles to this mixture  profile so  you could have various explanations for  example  melissa and marie the two individuals in  question were both heterozygous  contributing 1415  they were both homozygous one  contributing the 1414  one contributing the 1515 or  some variability where one was  heterozygous and the other was  homozygous  really looking at the totality of the  dna profile to understand  how each individual contributed their  dna to that mixture  in contrast massively parallel  sequencing because of the  informativeness in that sequence  diversity then allows you to  make an elimination of one possibility  and to better understand  the quantitative nature of the profile  so here we can eliminate that both  melissa and marie were homozygous  simply because we know that that no  longer holds true  based on the sequence data and we also  gain some information about  how the alleles are quantified in that  mixture  again potentially eliminating additional  possibilities  so what we know is that massively  parallel sequencing is a promising  technology for forensic genetics  and that we will be able to gain a  substantial amount  of str variation knowledge using this  approach  so i just want to spend the next few  slides talking  about a characterization study that was  conducted to look at  our commonly used strs to understand how  sequence diversity can be of use in  forensic genetics  excuse me so we looked at sequence  variation  for 59 strs 27 autosomal seven  xstr's and 25 y chromosome strs  and this included all of the coracotis  loci  we performed this characterization study  in 777  individuals across four major population  groups  and we utilized the forensic library  preparation kit  in combination with the my seek fgx  forensic genomic system  and we performed testing or our analyses  using both the manufacturer software as  well as a variety of in-house tools  the main goals of this study were really  to examine  what sequence diversity existed in the  commonly used  strs and how significant the sequence  diversity was  further we wanted to understand the  differences between the repeat region  and the flanking region  sequence diversity and how those  two types of variation might come  together to make a locus  or a location in the dna particularly  heterozygous  or diverse we also wanted to observe if  there were any particular trends  in the data whether certain loci were  more  heterozygous using a sequence based  approach and we performed a variety of  population genetic tests to ensure that  the data  were conforming to our expectations and  could be utilized in a forensic setting  we also wanted to identify any  shortcomings or limitations that could  be problematic for other research groups  but ultimately we wanted to just  generate a compendium of data that could  be used  by the forensic community as we move  forward into this new area  what we found in this really extensive  study was  four main trends first we saw an  increase in alleles for loci  when looking at only the repeat region  second  we saw an increase in alleles for  certain loci looking only at the  flanking regions so that region  outside of the repeat region third we  saw an increase in alleles in certain  loci  when combining the repeat and the  flanking regions  and then the fourth scenario was that we  saw no increase in alleles when  comparing to a traditional pcrce  approach or a length based approach  showing you the data and how we  visualized it to fully understand  sequence variation we have a locus here  and you can see that everything is  color-coded  where each nucleotide has a different  color  but we could easily visualize any trends  um or any snip variation that might be  present in the data  any black dots are deletions or missing  data  in that particular string sequence if we  zoom  in you can see in the orange box we have  the flanking region  and then at the top you have that repeat  region start  and you can see in that first repeat for  this particular locus  there's quite a bit of sequence  diversity  present and this sequence diversity has  been characterized  in the literature and so it wasn't  uncommon to observe it  but essentially tabulating the data in  this way allowed for easy recognition  of sequence variations that we could  best understand  each locus that we were studying  what this study really revealed and why  we thought it was very significant to  share with the forensic community  is that there's a substantial gain in  information using a sequence based  approach  so you can see here the y chromosome the  x chromosome  and the autosomal strs all showed  an extensive gain in information using  sequence-based alleles  demonstrated in orange over length-based  alleles demonstrated in purple  focusing in on d9s1122  i really want to point out how  significantly impactful  sequence variation can be so we're going  to focus  in on the purple bar which is the allele  12.  zooming in you can see that there are  three different  alleles sequence based alleles  represented for the length-based allele  12.  the motif defined in the middle is  referring simply to that repeat region  and if you shift your attention to the  motif with flanking region sequences  you can see that highlighted in red and  highlighted in yellow  is the uniqueness of adding in that  flanking region information  so we went from one length based allele  12 to now  three sequence based allele  12.  breaking this down for you the 12 length  based allele will present  as a peak single peak 12 on an  electroferogram  however if you look at the sequence you  can see that there's quite a bit of  variability  looking only at the repeat region you  have two unique alleles  so the taga 12 or you have the  taga1tcga1  tag10 and then you gain an additional  allele when you add in that flanking  region information  so this is an example of a locus that  when using both the repeat  and the flanking region information you  have a substantial gain in the total  number of alleles present  what's interesting is that looking at  the totality of the d9 locus we went  from  eight length-based alleles to 17  sequence-based alleles so that's a  drastic improvement in the overall power  of discrimination of those alleles at  that locus  visualizing what i just explained and  going back to that initial  image of sequence variation from the  beginning of my talk you can see here  where that variability falls within  the locus so typically  that snip is found at the beginning of  the repeat region  so it's highlighted in blue and you can  see it with the purple arrow  and that flanking region variation is  characterized by the orange arrow and  does have that db snip  record associated with it meaning that  it has been observed by other groups  so what can happen when you only look at  the repeat region because  we spend all of this time really  focusing on characterizing  alleles based on their repeat region  repeat region composition  remembering that pcr ce  captures that entire amplicon so those  primers are set outside of the repeat  region capturing flanking region  information on either side of the repeat  even though we don't report it that way  we're still capturing that information  and changes in the flanking region will  impact the  actual length-based allele call if  there's an  insertion a deletion etc  when we think about using mps and if we  only focus on the repeat region we're  potentially  losing that important information that's  captured  and quantified in the flanking region  so as we move forward using an nps based  approach it's very important to be  consistent with our pcrce methods of  looking at the entire amplicon and  defining both the repeat and the  flanking region  in terms of what the totality of allele  composition is  so just a couple of examples to really  reinforce this point  looking at d7 if you look at the repeat  region  allele 11 by length  only the mps software so in this case we  use the forensic universal analysis  software  reports only tatc 11.  however when doing concordance studies  with pcr  ce we found that it was at 10.3 and  only in looking at the flanking region  information were we able to capture a  deletion  in the five prime flank in order to  explain  this micro variant or the 10.3 allele  designation  so again just reinforcing the value of  looking at the totality of information  captured  another example looking at d22 the 15  allele  the forensic software denoted just the  repeat region calling it a 15  whereas concordance data with capillary  electrophoresis and then using our  in-house tools  demonstrated that it was actually a 15.1  because of an insertion in the five  prime flank  of the amplicon so again just  stressing the value and importance of  looking at the totality of the amplicon  information  as i come to a close i want to just  bring your attention to the population  genetic consequences  of using an mps-based approach  really we're gaining so much information  in terms of the diversity of alleles  using an nps based approach  but it will also strengthen our  statistical support  for our profiles in terms of the rarity  of particular alleles and then the  rarity of the overall profile  and then looking at population specific  differences and inferring potential  ancestral information  from the unique sequences or the  the rare alleles that we're capturing in  actual casework so here i presented just  looking at the overall  random match probability of the  autosomal loci from a length versus a  sequence based approach  and you can see that there are multifold  differences  within each of our four populations  using either  approach taking that a step further  there are obvious markers that either  drastically increase the diversity  of a dna profile and have a significant  impact on the rarity  of that profile and therefore impact the  random match probability and there are  other loci where we learned  didn't have any real advantage using a  sequence based approach  and so they didn't significantly change  the power of discrimination  for their alleles in a dna profile and  those are highlighted here  so for a sequence based improvement  you have certain alleles highlighted in  green that are really going to  take your dna profile to the next level  in terms of power of discrimination  and then highlighted in red  where there is no significant  gain in information using those loci  because their length-based designations  seem to be  sufficiently powerful or  sufficiently useful for inferring  the rarity of those alleles  some final thoughts just pulling all of  this information together  we know that using a length-based  approach pcrce is a robust way to  generate a forensic dna profile  however as we continue to push the  boundaries of our sequence-based  research  it's very important to keep in mind that  a sequence based approach  does offer significant promise and  potential for advancement in our field  of forensic genetics  looking at the overall improvement of  diversity or variation at particular  loci  in the study that i talked about in this  presentation  we saw an increase in 644 alleles using  a sequence-based approach  and that is extremely significant that  just speaks to how much information that  we're not  capturing using pcr ce of those 400 were  novel  meaning that they were never yet or they  were never before  characterized in the literature so that  was a really  wonderful learning opportunity for our  group to really understand  the amount of diversity that hasn't yet  been  captured in the forensic genetic  research  and then finally it allowed us to  identify that certain loci aren't  substantially  more powerful using a sequence based  approach and that potentially we have  the opportunity to explore  new loci that might better aid us  in our complex interpretations moving  forward  but um as the literature has supported  there have been continued efforts to  characterize  our forensic loci using a sequence-based  approach  and there is a compendium of data  including the starseek  database housed by the national  institute of standards and technology  available  to really understand the sequence  diversity in our loci  a great um publication that highlights  what i discussed  in today's talk um came out in 2016.  so please check out that publication if  you're interested in learning more about  exactly what our study entailed and just  to provide you with a variety of  resources  in better understanding sequence  diversity  and i'd just like to thank my team  at the university of north texas health  science center  where i completed my doctoral studies  and did  the totality of the data presented in  this talk  as well as illumina and the national  institute of justice who supported this  research  if you have any questions please feel  free to reach out to me  i am again at the university of toronto  mississauga  um working in the forensic science  program and i'm very happy to talk more  about  str sequence variation forensic genetics  or forensic biology in general  thank you
 

